maria_dev: Heads up — trying to run the new JS worker example with Wrangler and it's choking on `require` + Node built-ins. Error: `ReferenceError: require is not defined` then `Error: Cannot find module 'fs'`. Example currently imports node-fetch and uses Buffer.

samir_dx: That's expected. Cloudflare Workers are ESM and have no Node APIs. The example slipped through as a Node-oriented CJS bundle. We need an edge/browser build that uses globalThis.fetch and Web Crypto, not node-fetch or Buffer.

ida_engineer: Quick fix locally: set package.json to \"type\": \"module\", switch to `import`, drop Buffer usage, and remove any `process.env` reads. For secrets in Workers use Wrangler's secrets (they're available via env bindings) or pass them into the client constructor.

maria_dev: I tried that. Typescript still fails — our distributed d.ts references `NodeJS.ReadableStream` and `Buffer`. Worker projects complain during tsc compile. How should we ship types for both Node and edge?

alex_docs: We should generate two sets of types: dist/types/node/index.d.ts (Node types) and dist/types/edge/index.d.ts (DOM types). Then document which to reference. Also update package.json exports to point import -> edge ESM, require -> node CJS.

samir_dx: For runtime we can add a small detection helper: `const isEdge = typeof globalThis.process === 'undefined' && typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function'`. Also accept an injected `fetch` and `getSecret` in the client constructor so platform specifics are pluggable.

ida_engineer: I pushed an edge adapter sketch in my branch:```js
// edge-client.js
export function createEdgeClient({ apiKey, fetchImpl = globalThis.fetch }) {
  return async function call(payload) {
    const res = await fetchImpl('https://api.redwood-inference.example/v1/generate', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Authorization': `Bearer ${apiKey}` },
      body: JSON.stringify(payload),
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error('upstream error');
    return await res.json();
  };
}
```

maria_dev: That avoids node deps — nice. But CI flagged bundle size on staging: build size 1.08MB > 1MB limit for the worker. We need to drop polyfills and tree-shake heavier libs.

ci-bot: build:example-worker failed — size 1.08MB (limit 1MB).

samir_dx: Remove any Buffer/polyfill shims, don't bundle dev-only helpers. Consider lazy-loading heavy utilities (not allowed in Workers though) or routing heavier features to Hosted API. Also ensure rollup/webpack target is `es2020` browser and avoid including full axios/node-fetch. Use native fetch.

alex_docs: Docs action: create an \"Edge Quickstart\" in examples that lists: 1) package.json type=module, 2) using wrangler secrets/env bindings, 3) using createEdgeClient with injected fetch/getSecret, 4) tsc config with `lib: [\"dom\", \"es2020\"]`. I'll draft it once the example PR lands.

ida_engineer: One more gotcha: some consumers still expect `global` (node) variable. We added `globalThis.global = globalThis;` in the example to placate old libs but that's hacky. Prefer removing global usage in SDK.

maria_dev: Good plan. Can we also add a CI step for the example that runs `wrangler publish --dry-run` or bundles with wrangler and `tsc --lib dom`? Catch these regressions early.

samir_dx: I'll take export map + dual-build. ida — can you open a PR for the edge adapter + smaller bundle strategy? alex — add the CI check to the example repo.

alex_docs: Will do. I'll also add an `edge.d.ts` to our types generation workflow so consumers on Workers don't get Node types.

maria_dev: Thanks! If we merge these this week I'll re-test with the customer sample app. :thumbsup:
